Json::parse can throw exception.
Use something like
try
    {
        data = Json::parse(jsonFile, nullptr, false);
    }
    catch (Json::parse_error& e)
    {

    }

Since the range is continuous, there is no need for the overhead of a map.

constexpr size_t cipherSuiteSize = 3;
constexpr size_t nrCipherSuites = 20;
static constexpr std::array<std::array<uint8_t, cipherSuiteSize>, nrCipherSuites> cipherSuites = {{...}, ...};

constexpr std::array<std::array<uint8_t, cipherSuiteSize>, nrCipherSuites>
        cipherSuites{{
            {RAKPA, RAKPI, RAKC},
            {RAKPA, HMAC_SHA1_96, RAKC},
            {RAKP_HMAC_SHA1, HMAC_SHA1_96, RAKC},
...
            {RAKP_HMAC_SHA256, HMAC_SHA256_128, AES_CBC_128},
            {RAKP_HMAC_SHA256, HMAC_SHA256_128, xRC4_128},
            {RAKP_HMAC_SHA256, HMAC_SHA256_128, xRC4_40}
            }};


Also, I am not sure we need to list out every cipher suite. I understand that they are all defined in the IPMI spec, but openbmc only supports 3 and 17, so this seems like overkill :)
